  "explanation": "The proposal investigates whether LLM-generated natural language explanations faithfully track minimal logical rule modifications or maintain persuasive post-hoc rationalizations. This builds directly upon existing evaluation frameworks testing explanation faithfulness via counterfactual input edits and logical reasoning stress tests. While applying single-condition rule perturbations to audit free-text explanation faithfulness is a well-motivated and timely empirical study, it overlaps closely with existing work evaluating counterfactual faithfulness and self-consistency in LLM explanations.",
  "problems": [
    "The core idea of probing explanation faithfulness via counterfactual perturbations is conceptually close to existing metrics like the Correlational Counterfactual Test (CCT) and self-consistency tests.",
    "The reliance on simple token-overlap and keyword matching to assess explanation shifts may fail to capture semantic equivalents or complex rephrasings of the perturbed rule condition."
  ],
  "improvements": [
    "Incorporate embedding-based semantic distance or NLI-based entailment models to measure whether the explanation logically incorporates the inverted rule condition rather than relying solely on token overlap.",
    "Expand the evaluation to include multi-step rule compositions rather than restricting perturbations strictly to single-condition propositional clauses."
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "passage_id": "s_82484666fca05d79f910",
      "claim": "Prior work introduces counterfactual input edits to evaluate the faithfulness of free-text explanations by measuring prediction distribution shifts.",
      "relation": "overlaps"
    },
    {
      "passage_id": "s_36997ac72a1fc214cb37",
      "claim": "Existing literature evaluates post-hoc and chain-of-thought explanations generated by LLMs to test whether they faithfully reflect internal reasoning.",
      "relation": "overlaps"
    }
  ],
  "uncertainty": "The search coverage is limited to retrieved ACL Anthology papers from recent years, meaning additional concurrent or very recent preprints evaluating rule-perturbation faithfulness may exist outside this snapshot.",
  "tradeoffs": [
    "Using a zero-shot prompting setup with a 7B model fits within the 1-2 consumer GPU resource constraints but limits generalization conclusions compared to evaluating larger proprietary models."
  ],
  "falsification_test": "If an open-source 7B model achieves near-perfect explanation shift accuracy on perturbed rules under simple zero-shot prompting, the hypothesis that LLMs maintain persuasive invariant rationalizations would be falsified for this model class."
}
